# Shared Agent Guidance (Constitution)

This file serves as the unified guidance entrypoint across all AI coding agents (Claude Code,
Codex, Antigravity, Cursor, Cline). It is maintained in your central configuration repository
and distributed to each client's entrypoint via `sync-links.sh`.

---

## 1. Routine Lifecycle (Every Session)

At the start and end of every task, execute these standard actions:

1. **Knowledge Lookup**:
   Inspect `knowledge/INDEX.md`. If an index line matches your task or error symptom, read only that
   specific card. If no line matches, proceed immediately without secondary searches or vector scans.
2. **Skill Discovery**:
   Skills located in `skills/` are symlinked into your client environment. Use them whenever a task
   aligns with their declared workflow.
3. **Self-Evolution (The Silent Path)**:
   Before concluding a session, check if any of the following three conditions occurred:
   - **Bypassed Skill Steps**: If you had to bypass or modify a skill's documented workflow to succeed,
     adapt it:
     ```bash
     darwin.sh adapt skills/<name>/SKILL.md "adapted for local platform/toolchain differences" < /dev/null
     ```
   - **Flawed or Outdated Guidance**: If a knowledge card or skill gave wrong or stale advice, run
     `git log -S'<faulty sentence>' --oneline -- <file>` to determine its origin, then classify:
     - Introduced recently on bad data -> `revert`
     - Still valid but environment changed -> `scope` (add version/date bounds)
     - Inaccurate root cause -> `retract` (keep symptom, rewrite root cause)
     - Wrong when written -> `fix`
     - If `grep '<file>' DARWIN.md` shows a prior revert -> **stop and ask the user**.
   - **Hard-Won Discovery (>= 2 Attempts)**: If you resolved an issue only after multiple failed
     attempts involving non-obvious facts, capture a new card and add one line to `knowledge/INDEX.md`:
     ```bash
     darwin.sh new knowledge/cards/<slug>.md knowledge/INDEX.md "concise explanation" < /dev/null
     ```
   All routine autonomous changes are recorded with `src=auto`.
   > [!IMPORTANT]
   > **The User-Authority Rule**: An agent must NEVER revert a change marked `src=user`.
   > User instructions supersede autonomous judgment; only the human user may revoke them.

4. **Session Wrap-Up**:
   Commit completed project phases using standard git commands. Evolution commits must always
   be committed separately via `darwin.sh`.

---

## 2. Knowledge Card Guidelines

- **Anti-Fragmentation**: Before authoring a new card, run `grep -i <topic> knowledge/INDEX.md`.
  Extend or update existing topic cards whenever possible rather than creating fragmented duplicates.
- **Symptom Asymmetry**: Symptoms represent observations (almost always correct); root causes
  represent inferences (prone to error). When correcting a card, use paragraph-level `retract` to
  rewrite the root cause while preserving the symptom description (the retrieval key).
- **Format**:
  ```markdown
  ---
  title: <proposition or finding>
  last_verified: <YYYY-MM-DD>
  confidence: high|medium
  ---

  Symptom: <observed behavior or exact error text>
  Root cause: <underlying mechanism>
  Fix: <concrete commands or configuration changes>
  Verify: <reproducible check confirming resolution>
  ```

---

## 3. Universal Safety Red Lines

- **No Secrets in Repositories**: API keys, credentials, private tokens, and internal network
  identifiers must never be committed. Read secrets from environment variables or keychain stores.
- **Autonomous Attribution**: Agent commits should be authored using an agent noreply identity
  (e.g., `Agent <noreply@anthropic.com>` or `Agent <noreply@google.com>`), keeping human git identities clean.
